etric: lines are newline characters, words are transitions from whitespace to non-whitespace, bytes are the raw count, characters are runes, and max line length accounts for tab expansion to multiples of 8.\n\nSince the design specifies byte-oriented whitespace detection under LC_ALL=C, I should use a simple byte check for the ASCII whitespace characters rather than relying on Go's Unicode-aware functions.\n\nFor the line length calculation with tabs, I need to track display width where tabs expand to the next multiple of 8, while other bytes count as single columns—though I'm still thinking through how to handle non-printable characters in this context. Looking at the actual GNU wc implementation, tabs advance to the next multiple of 8, carriage returns and newlines reset the line position to 0, and for other characters, only printable ones contribute to the width based on their wcwidth value—control characters add nothing to the displayed length. I need to verify my understanding against the actual test case. The test expects \"22\" as the maximum line length for the input \"short\\na much longer line here\\nmed\\n\", which means \"a much longer line here\" should count as 22 characters—so I'm on the right track with how printable ASCII characters contribute width.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll follow GNU wc's approach: track the current line width by counting printable characters (0x20-0x7E), handle tabs by advancing to the next multiple of 8, and update the maximum whenever a newline ends a line. Backspace doesn't need special handling since it's not printable under the C locale, so it contributes zero to the position. Now I'm thinking through the implementation approach—I'll use a buffered reader to process input in chunks rather than loading everything into memory, which is more efficient for large files or infinite stdin. For the initial version, processing byte-by-byte should be straightforward enough.\n\nWriting the counting function... Still writing the 