line position.\n\nWriting the input processing function... Finishing the word-counting logic...\n\nI need to handle the final line length at EOF when there's no trailing newline, then return the counter. I'm also realizing there's a subtle distinction in how the GNU implementation counts words—it increments the counter only when transitioning out of a word, not when entering one, which my current approach mirrors with the `inWord` flag logic. I'm noticing a conflict between the requirements: the PRD says -m should count Unicode code points, but the design decision restricts us to LC_ALL=C behavior where characters equal bytes. I need to reconcile these two constraints.\n\nSince the test suite only validates under LC_ALL=C and that's our explicit target, I'll implement -m to count bytes for now, which aligns with how GNU wc behaves in the C locale. If UTF-8 locale support becomes necessary later, I can refactor to use proper rune counting then.\n\nFor output formatting, I need to determine field width by checking if all file arguments are regular files—if so, I sum their sizes to calculate the width needed; otherwise I default to 7 digits. When processing stdin alone, since I can't stat it, I'll use the default width.\n\nFor the actual output, I'll print only the requested counts in their standard order (lines, words, chars/bytes, max-line-length), right-justified within the field width and separated by spaces, followed by the filename if applicable. If both -c and -m are specified, -m takes precedence.\n\nNow I need to work through the argument parsing to handle short options, long options, and the various file input modes. I need to handle the `--files0-from` flag to read NUL-delimited filenames from a file, which adds some complexity but is necessary for the implementation. I'm also looking at how GNU wc calculates field width for output formatting by examining the file statistics...\n\nExamining the width calculation logic...\n\nSo `number_width` initializes to 0