f the requirements—they only cover compilation, individual flags, default mode, and stdin handling. So this error case might not be tested in the differential test suite anyway since it requires specific file setup that the test framework doesn't handle. I'll focus on implementing the wc utility correctly without worrying about test files. Now I'm working on the `--files0-from` flag, which reads null-terminated filenames from a file or stdin and processes them accordingly. Simplifying the approach with a straightforward read and split on null bytes, then filtering out empty strings. I'm reconsidering whether to handle the edge case of consecutive nulls like GNU wc does, but since the test suite doesn't explicitly cover --files0-from edge cases and it's marked as out of scope, I'll keep the implementation straightforward.\n\nLooking at how stdin should work with the `-` argument—each occurrence reads whatever's currently available from stdin, so the first `-` consumes everything and subsequent ones get empty input, matching GNU wc's behavior. Now I need to write the full implementation, making sure to count files even when they fail to open so the totals are accurate.\n\nActually, let me verify the exact behavior when a file doesn't exist. GNU wc prints an error to stderr but doesn't output a line for that file—it just skips it in the output while still including it in the file count for the total line. Looking at how GNU wc handles file errors, when a file can't be opened it prints an error message to stderr and skips printing counts for that file, but still includes it in the total line calculation with zero contribution. So I need to make sure failed files don't get a count line printed, but they still trigger the total line to display if there are multiple file arguments. Continuing with the main loop logic...\n\nAfter accumulating the totals from successfully processed files, I print the per-file counts if needed, then output the total line if there are multiple