use `os.Args[0]` to get the actual invoked name, but for simplicity I'll just hardcode `\"wc\"` as the program name constant.\n\nI should also make sure `--total=only` still prints the total line even with a single file, and handle the stderr writes without worrying about write errors since GNU wc doesn't either.\n\nBuilding the main structure... I'm clarifying the behavior of `--files0-from` based on GNU wc's semantics: when combined with file arguments it should error, and when reading from a file or stdin it processes NUL-delimited filenames. Now I'm writing out the complete implementation starting with the package declaration and imports.\n\nWriting the main function and argument parsing... Setting up the file list to read from stdin if no files were provided, then calculating the display width for line numbers and determining whether to show per-file and total counts. Now I'm iterating through each file, processing it to get line/word/byte/character counts, accumulating those totals, and tracking any errors that occur. Defining the data structures...\n\nActually, I need to reconsider whether to track `chars` separately from `bytes`. The spec says to target LC_ALL=C behavior exclusively, where they're equivalent, so I could simplify by treating them as the same value rather than maintaining duplicate counts. So chars == bytes under C locale, which means I can simplify by tracking just bytes and using it for both -c and -m flags. The PRD does specify that -m should count Unicode code points for non-C locales, but since I'm targeting LC_ALL=C only, bytes and chars are equivalent. I'll keep them as separate fields in the struct for clarity and future-proofing, even though they'll have the same value.\n\nDefining the struct... Writing the output function...\n\nNow I need to implement the function that reads NUL-delimited filenames from a file or stdin, handling both standard input and file paths, then splitting the data by null bytes to extract the individual filenames.\n\nI'm also thinking through the `totalPolicy` function to determine whether to show per-file and total output based on the mode setting.\n\nNow I need to put everything together into the complete implementation. I'm also considering how the argument parser should handle various flag formats—like treating `-` as a file rather than a flag, supporting `--` to end flag parsing, combining short flags like `-lwc`, and accepting long flags with either `=` or space-separated values. Both `--total auto` and `--total=auto` should work, similar to how GNU wc handles them.\n\nWriting the argument parser... Handling short flags now, iterating through each character in the argument after the dash and setting the corresponding configuration flags like lines, words, bytes, and character count. Then I'm moving into the counting function that will process the input reader.\n\nBuilding the counting logic... Finishing up the word counting logic by handling the final word and line length at EOF, then returning the results. But I'm realizing there's an issue with my character classification in the default case—I need to reconsider which bytes actually get counted as word characters versus display width contributors. matches the GNU wc behavior under LC_ALL=C. The DEL character at 0x7F is correctly excluded from the printable range, which aligns with how the C locale treats it. Now I'm implementing the processFile function to handle both implicit stdin when no arguments are given and explicit stdin via the \"-\" argument.\n\nHandling file operations...\n\nI'm checking the error formatting to match GNU wc's output style. The error wrapping looks correct with the path and error message combined. I could use `*fs.PathError` from the `io/fs` package for better type safety in newer Go versions, or alternatively use `errors.Unwrap` to extract the underlying error more cleanly.\n\nActually, `*os.PathError` works fine since it's an alias for `fs.PathError` anyway, so I'll stick with that to