he os.Exit call not running defers, but I'm actually calling Flush explicitly before exiting, so that's fine—if the flush fails, I exit with code 1, otherwise with the accumulated exit code. Error messages written to stderr go directly to the unbuffered file descriptor, so those are handled immediately.\n\nThe file closes in processFile are deferred within that function's scope and complete before returning, so there's no issue there. Now I'm checking for any Go vet issues—all errors are being checked, no unused variables or imports, and the code style is consistent. I should verify that os.PathError is still valid in the current Go version rather than needing the newer *fs.PathError alias.\n\nOne thing to confirm: when I range over the flag string like `-lwc`, Go iterates over runes, which works fine for ASCII characters. Non-ASCII flags would just fall through to the default case and print an error, which is acceptable behavior.\n\nLet me review the acceptance criteria one more time to make sure the implementation covers everything. true. Output order in printLine is lines → words → bytes (correct). AC4: When no file arguments, files = [\"\"], which processes stdin. AC5: When 2+ files, totalPolicy returns showTotal=true with mode=auto. AC6: The code should pass vet. I'll verify.\n\nI need to ensure the cmd/wc/ directory exists, then write main.go. The test infrastructure is already set up, but my task is just the implementation file. I'm also thinking through the os.Exit calls in error handling to make sure they're placed correctly throughout the code.\n\nThe early exits during argument parsing are safe since no output gets written to the buffered writer yet. The actual flow is parse args first, then process files and write output, then flush. If a file can't be opened during --files0-from processing, that error gets printed and the exit code is set to 1, but we continue processing remaining files.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the implementation. Finishing the exit code logic...\n\nThe flush operation takes priority—if it fails, we exit with code 1 regardless of any earlier file errors. Otherwise, we respect the exitCode that was set during processing. I need to use `os.Exit` explicitly since Go's main always returns 0 by default, and I want to ensure the correct exit code is propagated to the shell.\n\nOne thing to watch: `os.Exit` bypasses deferred functions, but that's fine here since file cleanup happens within the processFile function scope and completes before we reach the exit call.\n\nNow I'm checking the printLine function to make sure the closure over the `first` variable works correctly...\n\nVerifying the format string behavior...\n\nActually, I should double-check how `\\v` (vertical tab) interacts with the `-L` flag. Looking back at the GNU wc implementation, vertical tab is treated as a word separator but doesn't affect line position tracking—it's not printable, so it doesn't advance the position counter. My current implementation handles this correctly by only processing the word separation logic for that case.\n\nFor `\\r` and `\\f`, they both reset the line position to zero and update the maximum line length before being treated as word separators, which my code also does correctly.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the implementation file, but I want to verify one more detail about error formatting—GNU wc outputs errors in the format `wc: filename: No such file or directory`, and I'm using `fmt.Errorf` to construct that message with the path and underlying error, which should produce the right format when printed. For read errors that occur during the counting process, the error handling works differently—the raw error from the Reader gets wrapped with the filename, so it would display as `wc: filename: [io error message]`. This approach keeps the error formatting consistent across both file-open failures and read failures.\n\nThe edge case of zero total size is handled correctly too—the width calculation defaults to 1 column, which is appropriate even when all files are empty, so the output would just show zeros across the board.\n\nNow I'm implementing the actual code.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3273,"cache_read_input_tokens":46893,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3273},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a115c45-1bdc-4b94-a522-324914dd00d1","uuid":"05ea61b1-638d-44be-ab68-b8631714d97b"}
